WEBVTT

00:00:00.324 --> 00:00:01.539
Last video ended here.

00:00:02.063 --> 00:00:04.005
On the left of the join, the gradient

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:06.108
goes minus one, zero,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.354
one, and keeps rising, so the curve bends

00:00:09.654 --> 00:00:09.895
upward.

00:00:10.596 --> 00:00:13.063
On the right, it stays at two, and

00:00:13.145 --> 00:00:14.067
the line doesn't bend at all.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.118
So the bend comes from how the gradient

00:00:17.158 --> 00:00:17.538
changes.

00:00:18.486 --> 00:00:20.557
Now fill this vase from a tap, at

00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:21.160
a steady rate.

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:23.910
The water level rises fast in the narrow

00:00:23.951 --> 00:00:24.211
base,

00:00:24.713 --> 00:00:27.682
slowly through the wide middle, then fast again

00:00:27.823 --> 00:00:28.705
in the narrow neck.

00:00:29.208 --> 00:00:31.315
So its graph bends down, then up.

00:00:32.462 --> 00:00:34.715
Where does the bend switch, and how do

00:00:34.736 --> 00:00:35.218
we measure it?

00:00:35.848 --> 00:00:38.194
Here's a formula that bends both ways too:

00:00:39.078 --> 00:00:41.965
y equals x cubed, minus six x squared,

00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:43.049
plus nine x.

00:00:43.712 --> 00:00:46.319
Its derivative, f prime, is a function in

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.102
its own right.

00:00:47.685 --> 00:00:50.236
It gives the gradient at every x, so

00:00:50.317 --> 00:00:51.683
we can differentiate it again.

00:00:52.348 --> 00:00:54.542
The result is called the second derivative.

00:00:55.086 --> 00:00:57.711
It's written f double prime of x, or

00:00:57.832 --> 00:01:00.216
d two y by d x squared, or

00:01:00.538 --> 00:01:01.278
y double prime.

00:01:02.122 --> 00:01:04.611
Here, f prime of x is three x

00:01:04.672 --> 00:01:06.940
squared, minus twelve x, plus nine.

00:01:07.543 --> 00:01:10.048
Differentiate again, and f double prime of x

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.813
is six x minus twelve.

00:01:12.536 --> 00:01:14.280
It tells us how fast the gradient is

00:01:14.321 --> 00:01:16.125
changing, and in which direction.

00:01:16.715 --> 00:01:19.206
Watch the tangent ride along the curve, with

00:01:19.227 --> 00:01:20.573
the gradient graph underneath.

00:01:21.437 --> 00:01:23.784
On the left, the gradient is falling, so

00:01:23.845 --> 00:01:26.733
the tangent keeps turning clockwise, and the curve

00:01:26.774 --> 00:01:27.395
bends down.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:29.352
That's called concave down.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.294
The curve sits below its tangents, like a

00:01:32.356 --> 00:01:32.596
cap.

00:01:33.622 --> 00:01:36.489
And concave down isn't the same as decreasing.

00:01:36.991 --> 00:01:39.844
This part rises, then falls, and it bends

00:01:39.885 --> 00:01:40.989
down the whole time.

00:01:41.773 --> 00:01:44.457
On the right, the gradient is rising, so

00:01:44.518 --> 00:01:47.443
the tangent turns anticlockwise, and the curve bends

00:01:47.564 --> 00:01:47.643
up.

00:01:48.248 --> 00:01:49.400
That's concave up.

00:01:50.087 --> 00:01:52.301
The curve sits above its tangents, like a

00:01:52.463 --> 00:01:52.684
cup.

00:01:53.529 --> 00:01:53.689
Now,

00:01:54.193 --> 00:01:56.362
f double prime is the gradient of this

00:01:56.443 --> 00:01:57.246
gradient graph.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.556
Where f prime is falling, f double prime

00:02:00.678 --> 00:02:01.258
is negative.

00:02:01.802 --> 00:02:04.217
Where f prime is rising, it's positive.

00:02:04.861 --> 00:02:07.646
So f double prime greater than zero means

00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:10.832
concave up, and less than zero means concave

00:02:10.853 --> 00:02:11.072
down.

00:02:11.821 --> 00:02:14.728
At x equals two, the gradient stops falling

00:02:14.848 --> 00:02:16.071
and starts rising.

00:02:16.753 --> 00:02:19.364
Its lowest value is minus three, right here.

00:02:20.128 --> 00:02:22.621
So the curve switches from concave down to

00:02:22.661 --> 00:02:23.425
concave up.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.741
A point where the concavity changes is called

00:02:26.761 --> 00:02:28.027
a point of inflection.

00:02:28.812 --> 00:02:30.700
It's at two, and f of two is

00:02:30.761 --> 00:02:31.001
two,

00:02:31.504 --> 00:02:32.970
so the point is two, two.

00:02:33.974 --> 00:02:36.180
Notice the tangent there crosses the curve.

00:02:36.703 --> 00:02:37.549
And it isn't flat.

00:02:38.275 --> 00:02:39.740
The gradient is minus three,

00:02:40.384 --> 00:02:42.753
so this inflection isn't a stationary point.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.094
The vase works the same way.

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:48.327
Its level rises slowest at the widest part,

00:02:48.729 --> 00:02:51.298
and that's exactly where its graph switches bend.

00:02:52.008 --> 00:02:54.599
Inflection candidates are where f double prime is

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.166
zero, or undefined.

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:59.674
Here, six x minus twelve is zero at

00:02:59.835 --> 00:03:00.555
x equals two,

00:03:01.178 --> 00:03:02.980
so check that the sign really changes.

00:03:03.903 --> 00:03:06.386
f double prime of one is minus six,

00:03:06.908 --> 00:03:09.252
and f double prime of three is plus

00:03:09.293 --> 00:03:09.552
six.

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:13.147
Minus to plus, so the concavity changes, and

00:03:13.348 --> 00:03:15.054
two, two is an inflection.

00:03:15.697 --> 00:03:18.021
So the cubic is concave down for x

00:03:18.242 --> 00:03:20.766
less than two, and concave up for x

00:03:20.907 --> 00:03:21.587
greater than two.

00:03:22.391 --> 00:03:22.974
Now compare

00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:25.007
y equals x to the fourth.

00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:28.442
f double prime is twelve x squared, which

00:03:28.524 --> 00:03:30.210
is zero at x equals zero.

00:03:30.753 --> 00:03:32.582
But it's positive on both sides.

00:03:33.185 --> 00:03:35.170
The curve is concave up all the way

00:03:35.211 --> 00:03:38.159
through, so there's no inflection at zero, even

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.185
though f double prime is zero there.

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:43.565
The concavity also classifies stationary points.

00:03:44.249 --> 00:03:46.669
Back on our cubic, f prime is three,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.591
times x minus one, times x minus three,

00:03:50.112 --> 00:03:52.592
so the tangent is flat at one and

00:03:52.773 --> 00:03:53.153
at three.

00:03:54.074 --> 00:03:56.381
At x equals one, f double prime is

00:03:56.462 --> 00:03:57.164
minus six.

00:03:57.686 --> 00:03:59.773
The curve is concave down there, so it

00:03:59.814 --> 00:04:02.444
sits below its flat tangent, and every nearby

00:04:02.545 --> 00:04:03.488
point is lower.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:06.617
That's a hilltop: a local maximum, at one,

00:04:06.838 --> 00:04:07.118
four.

00:04:07.942 --> 00:04:10.237
At x equals three, f double prime is

00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:10.539
six.

00:04:11.144 --> 00:04:13.652
Concave up, so the curve sits above its

00:04:13.693 --> 00:04:16.985
flat tangent: a local minimum, at three, zero.

00:04:17.768 --> 00:04:20.412
But if f double prime is zero at

00:04:20.453 --> 00:04:22.817
a stationary point, the test can't decide.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.523
Look at these three curves:

00:04:25.066 --> 00:04:25.748
x to the fourth,

00:04:26.311 --> 00:04:27.455
minus x to the fourth,

00:04:27.978 --> 00:04:28.821
and x cubed.

00:04:29.543 --> 00:04:31.807
All three have a flat tangent at zero,

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:34.512
and f double prime equals zero there.

00:04:35.155 --> 00:04:36.260
Yet one is a minimum,

00:04:36.763 --> 00:04:38.994
one is a maximum, and one is neither.

00:04:39.659 --> 00:04:41.893
So fall back on the signs either side.

00:04:42.598 --> 00:04:43.642
For x to the fourth,

00:04:44.165 --> 00:04:46.393
f prime goes minus, then plus:

00:04:46.897 --> 00:04:47.578
a local minimum.

00:04:48.182 --> 00:04:50.670
For minus x to the fourth, it's plus,

00:04:50.993 --> 00:04:52.899
then minus: a local maximum.

00:04:53.502 --> 00:04:56.152
For x cubed, f double prime is six

00:04:56.394 --> 00:04:58.703
x, which changes sign at zero.

00:04:59.246 --> 00:05:01.553
So the concavity changes at a flat point.

00:05:02.036 --> 00:05:03.984
That's a horizontal point of inflection.

00:05:04.716 --> 00:05:06.163
Here's a way to remember the signs.

00:05:06.787 --> 00:05:08.495
Positive bends up, like a cup.

00:05:09.121 --> 00:05:11.116
Negative bends down, like a cap.

00:05:11.923 --> 00:05:14.154
A cup's flat point is its bottom, a

00:05:14.195 --> 00:05:14.516
minimum.

00:05:15.039 --> 00:05:17.286
A cap's flat point is its top, a

00:05:17.328 --> 00:05:17.748
maximum.

00:05:18.292 --> 00:05:20.089
And zero means look again.

00:05:22.753 --> 00:05:23.201
Your turn.

00:05:23.728 --> 00:05:26.232
Find the stationary points of y equals x

00:05:26.313 --> 00:05:29.117
cubed, plus three x squared, minus nine x,

00:05:29.559 --> 00:05:31.882
and use the second derivative to determine their

00:05:31.903 --> 00:05:32.182
nature.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:33.721
Pause here to answer.

00:05:44.527 --> 00:05:47.548
y prime is three, times x plus three,

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.269
times x minus one,

00:05:49.791 --> 00:05:52.451
so the stationary points are at minus three

00:05:52.653 --> 00:05:53.152
and one.

00:05:53.996 --> 00:05:56.403
y double prime is six x plus six.

00:05:57.106 --> 00:05:59.811
At minus three, it's minus twelve, so concave

00:05:59.852 --> 00:06:02.898
down: a local maximum, at minus three, twenty-seven.

00:06:03.641 --> 00:06:06.692
At one, it's twelve, so concave up: a

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:08.881
local minimum, at one, minus five.

00:06:09.526 --> 00:06:11.781
Positive at one means a minimum, not a

00:06:11.822 --> 00:06:12.204
maximum.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:14.312
Last one.

00:06:15.058 --> 00:06:17.482
Find any points of inflection on the curve

00:06:17.964 --> 00:06:20.768
y equals x to the fifth, minus five

00:06:20.969 --> 00:06:21.629
x to the fourth.

00:06:22.456 --> 00:06:23.287
Justify your answer.

00:06:24.036 --> 00:06:24.822
Pause here to answer.

00:06:35.655 --> 00:06:38.659
y double prime is twenty x squared, times

00:06:38.861 --> 00:06:39.581
x minus three,

00:06:40.083 --> 00:06:42.647
so it's zero at zero and at three.

00:06:43.553 --> 00:06:44.178
Test the signs.

00:06:44.986 --> 00:06:46.438
At minus one, it's negative.

00:06:46.924 --> 00:06:48.341
At one, still negative.

00:06:48.848 --> 00:06:50.062
At four, positive.

00:06:50.906 --> 00:06:53.987
So the concavity changes only at three, and

00:06:54.008 --> 00:06:57.389
the only point of inflection is three, minus

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:58.450
one hundred and sixty-two.

00:06:59.376 --> 00:07:01.810
Zero isn't one, because the sign doesn't change

00:07:01.851 --> 00:07:02.011
there.

00:07:03.755 --> 00:07:06.158
So now we have three graphs that work

00:07:06.179 --> 00:07:09.384
together: the curve itself, its gradient f prime,

00:07:09.906 --> 00:07:11.808
and the second derivative, f double prime.

00:07:12.571 --> 00:07:14.720
Their signs tell you where the curve rises,

00:07:14.962 --> 00:07:17.111
where it falls, and which way it bends.

00:07:17.955 --> 00:07:20.078
For our cubic, f prime is zero at

00:07:20.279 --> 00:07:22.583
one and three, the turning points, and f

00:07:22.644 --> 00:07:25.569
double prime changes sign at two, the inflection.

00:07:26.493 --> 00:07:28.886
Next: put all three together, to sketch a

00:07:29.109 --> 00:07:29.711
whole curve.

00:07:32.301 --> 00:07:33.104
Here are the key notes.

00:07:33.747 --> 00:07:35.475
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:35.536 --> 00:07:36.018
to copy them.
